"13299051-2164-5d54-b90e-52c7a8f1d7f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8391"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d68a11bd-7a2d-5f1f-8be7-c8904fdfb3e7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505812}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "8ad7b4f1-5165-5f3a-b42e-2ad2eda221fc" :step/math-academy-id 31245 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093160}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092293 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092140 :question/id #uuid "4fbdc166-4225-5fa5-aaa2-0f09073e8e2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8768"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50501d8e-9c4c-5650-a50f-5d847d29435a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092931} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505825}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Inverse Hyperbolic Function From First-Principles"} :step/id #uuid "0ad68353-836a-50b6-ac4a-7e9b88a98c32" :step/math-academy-id 32687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093454 :tutorial/content "The hyperbolic functions have inverse functions that are easy to define. The **inverse hyperbolic functions** are the following:\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic sine**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\sinh^{-1}{x} = \\arsinh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given below.\n\n![](images/1f/1f07f38b11cf297e099341df2ab062d4691c4502098c060de565b70f1ea7e360.png)\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic cosine**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\cosh^{-1}{x} = \\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in [1, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given below.\n\n![](images/60/60607a764c50807dcb0f4f05328d900c626079654995cffbaa84fa2214788835.png)\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic tangent**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\tanh^{-1}{x} = \\artanh{x} = \\dfrac12\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}\\right), \\qquad x \\in (-1, 1).\n$$\n\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is given below.\n\n![](images/96/96b4f7aa06ee165751dcaa2101e9dea104d95d5bebdaea7bc4f317d9160b5a7a.png)\n\nFor example, to calculate $\\arcosh(\\sqrt2)$ we substitute $x$ for $\\sqrt2$ in the formula for $\\arcosh$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\sqrt{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{2-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "afeb7395-4188-5e5e-8d91-200a65a1cbb9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5206 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6f5b5a8c-0e08-5ad0-9503-4a131c8d5c2e" :step/math-academy-id 32690 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093455 :tutorial/content "We can derive the formulas from inverse hyperbolic sine, cosine, and tangent from the definition of these base functions. \n\nFor example, let's show that\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\cosh^{-1}{x} = \\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in [1, \\infty).\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cosh{x} = \\dfrac12 \\left( e^x + e^{-x} \\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, let $y = \\cosh{x}$ and $x \\geq 0.$ Then we have $y\\geq 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, now we have that $x \\geq 1$ and $y \\geq 0.$\n\nNext, we use the definition of $\\cosh x$ and bring everything to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{y}+{e}^{-y} \\\\ 2x{e}^{y} & ={e}^{2y}+1 \\\\ {e}^{2y}-2x{e}^{y}+1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{y})}^{2}-2x({e}^{y})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $Y = e^{y},$ we form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{y} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}\\leq 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{x^{2}-1}\\geq 1.$ Since $y \\ge 0,$ the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, so we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain our formula for $\\arcosh{:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x)=\n\\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "edf0d23e-aec4-5b77-befa-2433291ed5af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5219 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formulas for the Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "c7e64c95-cb61-5b1e-ac3c-639ae6f35144" :step/math-academy-id 32709 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093158}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Hyperbolic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092452}]}
